              "parentId": null,
              "displayOrder": 2,
              "marks": null,
              "isLeaf": false,
              "text": "A potential difference of 2.5 kV is applied across a pair of horizontal metal plates in a vacuum, as shown in Fig. 2.1. Each plate has a length of 5.9 cm. The separation of the plates is 4.0 cm. The arrangement produces a uniform electric field between the plates. Assume the field does not extend beyond the edges of the plates. An electron enters the field at point A with horizontal velocity 3.7 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ along a line mid-way between the plates. The electron leaves the field at point B.",
              "latex": "A potential difference of 2.5 kV is applied across a pair of horizontal metal plates in a vacuum, as shown in Fig. 2.1. Each plate has a length of 5.9 cm. The separation of the plates is 4.0 cm. The arrangement produces a uniform electric field between the plates. Assume the field does not extend beyond the edges of the plates. An electron enters the field at point A with horizontal velocity $3.7 \\times 10^{7}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ along a line mid-way between the plates. The electron leaves the field at point B.",
